  <section id="what-matters">
    <h2>What matters for a first board on a river</h2>
    <p>
      This is a buying guide, not a set of product reviews. We have not tested individual boards, so it does not rank brands. Instead it explains what to look for, based on general paddleboarding practice and manufacturers' own size guidance, so you can judge any board you are considering.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a beginner on a slow river, the priorities are stability, durability and ease of transport. Speed and glide matter far less than a board that stays under you while you learn to balance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="size">
    <h2>Board size and width</h2>
    <p>
      Volume and width decide how stable a board feels, and your weight and the load you add decide how much volume you need. As a rough guide, many all-round inflatable boards for adults are around 10 to 11 feet long, about 31 to 34 inches wide, and 5 to 6 inches thick. Heavier paddlers, or anyone carrying a child, a dog or a bag, generally want a bigger, wider board. Always check the manufacturer's stated maximum weight capacity and choose well within it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Width:</strong> a wider deck is more forgiving of wobbles. Narrower boards are faster but less stable.</li>
      <li><strong>Thickness:</strong> thicker inflatables are stiffer and float you higher, which helps with balance.</li>
      <li><strong>Length:</strong> longer tracks straighter on flat water; shorter turns more easily in a bendy river.</li>
    </ul>
    <img src="/images/blog/best-beginner-sup-uk/SUP_UK_Best_Beginner.png" alt="Beginner paddleboarder balancing on a wide, stable board on a calm river" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  </section>

