  <section id="inflatable">
    <h2>Inflatable or hard board?</h2>
    <p>
      Most beginners choose inflatables, because they pack into a bag, fit in a car boot and shrug off knocks. Quality inflatables use drop-stitch construction and, when fully pumped, feel very rigid. On a river an inflatable has one more advantage: if you hit a rock or log, it tends to bounce rather than crack.
    </p>
    <p>
      The downside is the very cheapest boards. Thinner materials, weaker seams and low pressure ratings can mean a board that flexes, tracks badly or fails sooner. A hard board performs better but needs roof racks and storage. For occasional river use, a mid-priced inflatable from a recognised brand with a clear warranty is usually the sensible starting point.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kit">
    <h2>What a starter kit should include</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Paddle:</strong> adjustable, ideally about 15 to 25 cm taller than you.</li>
      <li><strong>Pump:</strong> a double-action hand pump that can reach the board's recommended pressure, often around 12 to 15 PSI. Check the figure for your board.</li>
      <li><strong>Leash:</strong> but see the next section.</li>
      <li><strong>Repair kit and a rucksack</strong> for carrying the deflated board.</li>
      <li><strong>Buoyancy aid:</strong> often not included, but strongly recommended on rivers.</li>
    </ul>
    <img src="/images/blog/best-beginner-sup-uk/Aqua-Marina-SUP-Fusion.png" alt="Inflatable paddleboard kit laid out on grass" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  </section>

  <section id="leash">
    <h2>The river leash rule</h2>
    <p>
      A standard ankle leash keeps you attached to your board, which is helpful on the sea or a lake. In flowing water it can be dangerous, because the leash can snag on branches or rocks and hold you under. Paddle UK and other safety bodies advise using a quick-release waist belt leash on rivers, or no leash at all in some conditions, and learning to use the release. Ask a coach or your local club about the best setup for the water you plan to paddle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="before-buying">
    <h2>Before you buy</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Decide where you will paddle. Slow, flat stretches suit beginners; weirs and fast water do not.</li>
      <li>Check access and any licence you need, using <a href="/blog/do-i-need-a-license-to-paddleboard-on-a-river/">our licence and access guide</a>.</li>
      <li>Read <a href="/blog/river-kayaking-safety-for-beginners/">our river safety guide</a>, which applies to paddleboarders too.</li>
      <li>If you can, try a board through a club or rental session first. Many clubs run taster days.</li>
      <li>Compare warranty terms and the return policy, since fit and feel matter.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

